From 9f3d25c682bcbb891c1839e07855adec588089e8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 21:57:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] StereoscopicsManager: the eyes are drawn over/under where
 that gives frame packing

Frame packing is a layout of one eye above the other. A side-by-side
video, which Kodi draws side by side when the stereoscopic mode follows
the video, could only get the half side-by-side layout of a 3D display,
or none where the display has frame packing alone.

SetStereoMode() now asks the window system how to draw what is asked for
side by side (CWinSystemBase::GetStereoOutputArrangement()), and applies
over/under where the answer is that this gives frame packing with the
resolution in use. Nothing changes in the picture: the renderer cuts
each eye from the video by the layout of the video, whatever the layout
it is drawn in. The setting then shows "Over / Under".

A mode that the user picks in the settings is applied as it is, by
OnSettingChanged(): side by side stays side by side there.

ApplyStereoMode(), when nothing plays, chose the resolution by what
GetResInfo() says of the one in use. It now names that resolution, so
that it gets what the list says of it and not the frame of its 3D
output; without a 3D output the two are the same call.

For any mode but side by side, and where the window system has no 3D
output (every one but GBM, and GBM without a 3D display), SetStereoMode()
applies what it applied.

Not covered: the answer is the one for the resolution in use when the
mode is applied. A video that starts in a window and goes to full screen
later, at another refresh rate, keeps the way of drawing that was right
before.

Read and compiled (syntax check of StereoscopicsManager.cpp with the
commands of the last build). Not run.
---
 xbmc/guilib/StereoscopicsManager.cpp | 15 +++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/xbmc/guilib/StereoscopicsManager.cpp b/xbmc/guilib/StereoscopicsManager.cpp
index 4fdf28d..3de09e8 100644
--- a/xbmc/guilib/StereoscopicsManager.cpp
+++ b/xbmc/guilib/StereoscopicsManager.cpp
@@ -131,6 +131,14 @@ void CStereoscopicsManager::SetStereoMode(const RenderStereoMode mode)
   if (mode == RenderStereoMode::AUTO)
     applyMode = GetStereoModeOfPlayingVideo();
 
+  // Hades: a display takes frame packing, the layout that keeps every line of both eyes, only
+  // with the eyes one above the other. Asked for side by side, they are drawn one above the
+  // other where the window system says that this gives frame packing. The picture is the
+  // same: each eye is cut from the video by the layout of the video, whatever the layout it
+  // is drawn in.
+  if (applyMode == RenderStereoMode::SPLIT_VERTICAL)
+    applyMode = CServiceBroker::GetWinSystem()->GetStereoOutputArrangement(applyMode);
+
   if (applyMode != currentMode && applyMode >= RenderStereoMode::OFF)
   {
     if (CServiceBroker::GetRenderSystem()->SupportsStereo(applyMode))
@@ -513,8 +521,11 @@ void CStereoscopicsManager::ApplyStereoMode(const RenderStereoMode mode, bool no
       const auto appPlayer = components.GetComponent<CApplicationPlayer>();
       if (appPlayer && !appPlayer->IsPlaying())
       {
-        const RESOLUTION_INFO curInfo =
-            CServiceBroker::GetWinSystem()->GetGfxContext().GetResInfo();
+        // Hades: what the list says of the resolution in use, which is what a resolution is
+        // chosen by. (While that resolution has a 3D output, GetResInfo() without a
+        // resolution tells of the frame of that output.)
+        const RESOLUTION_INFO curInfo = CServiceBroker::GetWinSystem()->GetGfxContext().GetResInfo(
+            CServiceBroker::GetWinSystem()->GetGfxContext().GetVideoResolution());
         const bool is3D = (mode != RenderStereoMode::OFF && mode != RenderStereoMode::MONO);
         RESOLUTION res = CResolutionUtils::ChooseBestResolution(
             curInfo.fRefreshRate, curInfo.iScreenWidth, curInfo.iScreenHeight, is3D);
